  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Elevated wartime funding reallocates public procurement and R&amp;D subsidies toward defense and dual‑use AI compute, spurring government‑backed capital investment in AI infrastructure and related private co‑investment.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">china_us_tech_trade</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::wartime_funding_flow" target="global_bond_turmoil::nominal_treasury_yields" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Sustained increases in wartime funding needs (direct aid and defence spending) force higher sovereign debt issuance in key funding currencies, expanding supply and pushing up nominal Treasury yields as markets price additional issuance and financing premia.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">global_bond_turmoil</data>
</edge>
